WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.120
For all of the good that Superman's done — and,
over the past 80 years, he's done a lot of

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.569
good — he also can be kind of obnoxious.

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:12.059
But there's a difference between passive-aggression
and outright supervillainy, and while Superman

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:14.890
rarely crosses it, he often toes the line.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:18.850
Sometimes, Superman thinks he's acting in
humanity's best interest, and just makes a

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.310
bad judgement call.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.200
Other times, he's pushed to extremes.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.460
"Bad dog!"

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.380
Occasionally, it's mind-control or some alternate
dimension zaniness.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:40.080
And every now and then, as rare as it is,
the Man of Steel just gets downright nasty.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.620
Here are just a few of the worst things Superman
has ever done.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.800
Get out of town

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.340
When Superman first appeared, he was less
of the inspirational figure that fans know

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.760
and love today, and more of a rough-and-tumble
progressive who tackled social issues.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.180
In his first appearance in Action Comics #1,
Superman declares himself "the champion of

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.000
the oppressed" and spends most of his time
fighting crooked landlords and domestic abusers.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.640
By Action Comics #8, Superman tackles juvenile
delinquency by… getting Metropolis bombed?

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.570
After the local underprivileged teens don't
seem too interested in reforming from their

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:21.570
criminal ways, Superman exhibits an extremely
flawed understanding of basic sociology, and

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.230
blames these kids being jerks on their ramshackle
homes.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:30.130
With the true culprit clearly identified,
Superman is inspired by a devastating Florida

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:34.830
hurricane and goes to work, demolishing the
slums with his bare hands.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:39.409
This catches the attention of the army, who
inadvertently finish Superman's job by dropping

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:41.040
bombs on the Man of Steel.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.130
Ultimately, the government replaces the slums
with a series of nice, modern apartment buildings,

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.350
thereby gentrifying the neighborhood and forcing
Metropolis' poor out of town.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.640
But hey, at least crime is down, right?

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.450
Hitting a nerve

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.840
While growing up in Smallville, Clark Kent
had two close friends: Pete Ross, who knew

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:04.080
Clark was secretly Superboy, and Lana Lang,
his on-again off-again love interest.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.740
Pete ended up becoming proof that even if
you're best friends with the most powerful

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.810
being in the solar system, your life can still
fall apart pretty quickly.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:18.670
In DC Comics Presents #13, Superman runs across
the Nyrvnians, an advanced warrior race of

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.760
conquesting aliens who abduct Pete Ross' son
Jonathan, and it's up to Superman to rescue

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.760
him.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.760
Easy stuff, right?

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.900
But before Superman can make his move, the
Legion of Superheroes arrive from the future

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.480
and tell the Man of Steel to back off.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.850
Little Jonathan Ross is destined to become
Nyrvn's greatest warrior, instrumental to

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.450
the course of future events and galactic peace.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.540
It all adds up, and Superman leaves the kid
with the scary aliens.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.420
Needless to say, Poppa Pete doesn't take Superman's
news very well.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.790
He goes berserk, and eventually ends up locked
away in a mental institution, spending all

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.780
of his time ripping up posters of Superman.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.330
Baby bumped

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:00.730
It's pretty obvious that the bodies of humans
and Kryptonians are very different.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.760
They may look the same, but Superman can fly
and carry cars around without breaking a sweat;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.500
we grunt with exhaustion when we reach for
the remote on the other side of the couch.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.099
And when it comes to Superman and Lois Lane
making little half-human, half-alien babies…

00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:18.239
those conversations are best left to the sweaty
back room of the comic shop.

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:22.380
Well, there… and Adventures of Superman
Annual #3.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.720
In the story, a mysterious being named Waverider
takes a glimpse into Clark Kent's future,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.830
and makes a shocking discovery: after Lois
and Clark get married, their unborn child

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.730
kills Lois by giving her a superpowered womb
kick.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:37.730
Seriously.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:42.390
In his despair, Superman flees the planet,
essentially attempting suicide.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:44.130
It doesn't work, of course.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:49.550
After getting blasted by an alien missile,
Superman is rescued by Maxima, an alien warrior

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.500
princess who has the hots for him.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.730
Their eventual romance leads to an alien invasion
of Earth, and Superman leaving Earth for good…

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:01.150
but not until he makes out with his new girlfriend
on his wife's fresh grave.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.220
Classy.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.800
Take it sleazy

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:09.210
Years before Paris Hilton's naked night-vision
romp made the front page, Superman tried to

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.540
make the celebrity sex tape a thing, with
a little help from one of Darkseid's former

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.049
lieutenants, the aptly named Sleez.

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:22.079
Sleez, by the way, was banished from his home
planet by the most evil being in the universe...for

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:23.819
being too much of a perv.

00:04:23.819 --> 00:04:30.779
In Action Comics #592, Superman tracks fellow
hero Big Barda to Metropolis' very own Suicide

00:04:30.779 --> 00:04:36.229
Slum, where Superman discovers that Barda
been brainwashed by Sleez to produce a line

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:38.449
of popular adult films.

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:43.330
Superman tries to rescue Barda but ultimately
falls under Sleez's control too.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:48.879
And Sleez does exactly what you'd expect:
he takes Barda and Superman to the local pornographer

00:04:48.879 --> 00:04:50.930
and makes some naughty videos.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.050
Then Big Barda's husband shows up.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:58.389
To Superman's credit, his sense of morality
is pretty strong even while under mind control,

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:02.330
so their production doesn't make it much further
than the opening moments, and the director

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:06.080
complains that Superman has "all the sex appeal
of a side of beef."

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.949
Still, the sight of his wife sucking face
with the Man of Steel is enough to set Mr.

00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:15.590
Miracle off, and he frees both heroes from
Sleez's mind control.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:20.059
Neither one totally knew what happened, and
neither one wanted to talk about it, but thankfully,

00:05:20.059 --> 00:05:24.089
no Superman-looking babies popped up in the
DC Universe nine months later.

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:26.050
Murder and madness

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:30.180
Like lots of superheroes, Superman has a strict
no-murder policy.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.219
But how did he arrive at that stance?

00:05:32.219 --> 00:05:38.419
The answer lies in Superman #22, one of the
most controversial Superman stories ever told.

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:42.949
Superman finds himself in a pocket dimension
where a trio of Kryptonian soldiers — including

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:48.539
an alternate-reality version of General Zod
— have wiped out all life on Earth, except

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:53.800
for Bruce Wayne, Lex Luthor, and a synthetic
life form who goes by Supergirl.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:58.580
Superman and his allies put up a valiant fight,
but Superman is forced to take drastic measures.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.439
First, Superman robs them of their powers
using Gold Kryptonite, but that's not enough.

00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:08.089
Powered or not, Zod vows to track Superman
down and destroy his Earth.

00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:09.610
Superman doesn't take the threat lightly.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:13.210
He decides abandoning the villains on a dead
world isn't enough.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.129
He has to kill them.

00:06:15.129 --> 00:06:19.210
Superman looks on as the three Phantom Zone
criminals beg for their lives in the face

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.979
of deadly green Kryptonite, then returns home.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:27.069
Eventually, a guilty Superman decides to never
kill again, but it's too late for Zod.

00:06:27.069 --> 00:06:30.659
The damage has been done — mostly to Superman's
mind.

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:33.029
In the following days, he's plagued by guilt.

00:06:33.029 --> 00:06:36.300
He's not sleeping well and blacking out in
the middle of missions.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:41.899
Meanwhile, a masked crimefighter named Gangbuster
has been picking up Superman's slack, using

00:06:41.899 --> 00:06:43.339
much harsher methods.

00:06:43.339 --> 00:06:48.719
Unlike Superman, Gangbuster has no problem
beating goons within an inch of their lives.

00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:54.460
The vigilante's one-man war on crime ultimately
pits Gangbuster against the Guardian.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.159
If you've read comics before, it's not hard
to see where this is going.

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:03.969
In Adventures of Superman #450, Gangbuster's
costume is ripped in half, revealing Superman's

00:07:03.969 --> 00:07:05.360
suit underneath.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.520
As it turns out, Superman's guilt drove him
a little nuts and split his personality in

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:11.520
two.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.749
Confused and angry, Superman heads to space
for a few months to clear his head — proving

00:07:15.749 --> 00:07:19.849
without a doubt that his real superpower is
not having to keep a steady job.

00:07:19.849 --> 00:07:21.189
Thanks for watching!

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:23.770
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:25.560
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

